markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Variables

#### Have you ever noticed how a game keeps track of your score or how an app shows your number of likes? 

#### Programs do this using **variables!** Just like we use names to keep track of things in real life, programs use **variables** to keep track of changing information.

## What is a variable?

<div style="width:90%; margin:auto; font-family:sans-serif;">

  <!-- Row 1 -->
  <div style="display:flex; margin-bottom:10px;">
    <!-- Left Box -->
    <div style="flex:2; background-color:#f0f0f0; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); padding:10px; margin-right:10px;font-size:14pt;">
      <strong>Definition:</strong>
      <p>A <b>variable</b> is a name you give to a piece of data in your program. The value connected to that name can change while the program runs — you can think of it like a container that holds the data.</p>
      <strong>Example:</strong>
      <p> In a game, <b>score = 0</b> starts at zero and increases whenever a player earns points.
</p>
    </div>
    <!-- Right Box -->
    <div style="flex:1; background-color:#fff; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); padding:10px; text-align:center;">
      <img src="https://images.code.org/6aac191fc61f33592100e3d59ac7b717-image-1762187266713.png" alt="Eboxes symbolizing variable storage for greeting, animal, and age" style="width:60%; border-radius:10px;">
    </div>
  </div>
  
  

## What is state?

  <!-- Row 2 -->
  <div style="display:flex; margin-bottom:10px;">
    <div style="flex:2; background-color:#f0f0f0; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); padding:10px; margin-right:10px;font-size:14pt;">
      <strong>Definition:</strong>
      <p><b>State</b> is the current stored values of variables that describe what’s happening in a program right now.</p>
      <strong>Example:</strong>
      <p>In a game, the player’s current score and level together show the program’s state.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex:1; background-color:#fff; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); padding:10px; text-align:center;">
      <img src="https://images.code.org/e3a6cd792666963257437c276c283ed5-image-1762187492236.png" alt="A graph showing score, level, lives as examples of a programs state." style="width:60%; border-radius:10px;">
    </div>
  </div>

## What is an operation?

  <!-- Row 3 -->
  <div style="display:flex;">
    <div style="flex:2; background-color:#f0f0f0; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); padding:10px; margin-right:10px; font-size:14pt;">
      <strong>Definition:</strong>
      <p>An operation is an action that changes the value of a variable using math or logic.</p>
      <strong>Example:</strong>
      <p>When you click a “like” button, the operation score = score + 1 adds one to the total likes.
</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex:1; background-color:#fff; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); padding:10px; text-align:center;">
      <img src="https://images.code.org/2d7424f5c8832b0c3266d95848076aa7-image-1762187634384.png" alt="Graph showing a +1 operation changing the score from 4 to 5" style="width:50%; border-radius:10px;">
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

    
<br>  

---

## Let's see what this looks like in code:

#### This code defines the variables using the keyword `let`.

#### In JavaScript, a variable must be declared, or defined, before it can be used. 

<p style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/3fc90531df0a250a6d4d032641296c12-image-1762197748560.png" 
       alt="JavaScript variable example" 
       style="width:600px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
</p>


### Here, an **event handler** increases the `strength` variable by 2 each time the **strength-up** button is clicked.


<p style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/0c92bf9c223b25e95f34a943d8f26407-image-1762198022750.png" 
       alt="Event handler example" 
       style="width:600px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
</p>

## Remember:

#### There are always two steps when working with variables:

- #### 1. Define the variable (give it a name and a starting value).

- #### 2. Use or update the variable within the program’s actions or logic.


## Let's practice! 

#### In the next level, you will use AI to help debug a program with variables. 
MARKDOWN
